# Risk-accepted findings only — every entry here must have a reason.
#
# CVE-2026-45447 (libssl3t64, cartservice's final stage):
# cartservice's final stage is a "chiseled" distroless-style Ubuntu image
# (mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/runtime-deps:10.0.0-noble-chiseled) with no
# package manager, so this OS-level CVE can't be patched with apt. The
# FROM line already tracks the tag without a digest pin (see that
# Dockerfile) so it picks up Microsoft's fix automatically the next time
# they republish this image with a patched libssl3t64 — remove this line
# once Trivy stops flagging it.
CVE-2026-45447

# CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools), CVE-2026-24049 (wheel), GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
# (msgpack), CVE-2026-23949 (jaraco.context) — recommendationservice,
# emailservice, loadgenerator, shoppingassistantservice (all python:3.14.7
# base images):
# setuptools/wheel ship bundled with the base Python image's own install
# step, and jaraco.context/msgpack are vendored inside setuptools itself.
# An explicit `pip install --upgrade` in the Dockerfile does install a
# newer copy (confirmed: newer wheel/setuptools dist-info now present
# alongside the old one) but does not remove the base image's original
# bundled copy, so Trivy still flags the old one as present. Actually
# removing it would require deleting specific dist-info directories by
# exact path inside the base image before installing the replacement —
# fragile, base-image-version-specific surgery for build-tooling packages
# that aren't runtime-exposed attack surface in a deployed service. Risk
# accepted; revisit if a future python:3.14 base image ships these fixed
# by default.
CVE-2025-47273
CVE-2026-24049
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2026-23949
